    {
      "@id": "item/trash-view",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 29,
      "name": "A Trash page with restore and purge",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/15",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/files-actions-ui",
        "item/topbar-shell",
        "item/trash-directory",
        "item/trash-purge-manual"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "Another user's trashed items never appear, asserted at query level; restore and purge are wired to the actions. Files get Restore and Purge; directories get Restore ONLY -- a directory purge is out of scope here and remains issue #86, because files.directory_id cascades on delete, so force-deleting a directory row removes descendant file rows in SQL without ever reaching Services\\DocumentStorage, orphaning every object beneath it. decision/0031 folded #86 into this clause and the text was never amended; it is recorded here at completion -- a constraint the build already honoured, not one relaxed to fit.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T02:45:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T03:25:08Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/147"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/download-missing-object",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 30,
      "name": "A missing object is a stated answer, not a crash",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/134",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:6-storage-and-period-partitioning"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/download-reaches-the-browser"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "Both download controllers resolve the object BEFORE any header is sent -- DocumentStorage reports a missing object through a typed exception rather than a bare RuntimeException raised from inside the streamDownload callback, where the status line has already been decided and the observed code is an accident of when the exception surfaces relative to the header flush. Each route answers a deliberate status with a body and a log line naming the object key, so an operator can tell 'object storage is missing this object' from 'doccum crashed'. A test deletes the faked object behind a live row and asserts the status, the message and the log entry, for the current-version route and the per-version route both; the chosen status is stated in the docblock with its reasoning. The presigned path answers MinIO's own 404 and is left as is, noted rather than fixed. This matters because /data is the only volume and the backup story is 'copy /data': a restore that brings the database but not objects/ is the likeliest operator mistake in the product, and item/self-hosting-docs must be able to say what happens then.",
      "actionStatus": "CompletedActionStatus",
      "startTime": "2026-09-18T03:35:00Z",
      "endTime": "2026-09-18T03:53:32Z",
      "result": [
        "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/pull/152"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/ledger-mutation-nodes",
      "@type": "Action",
      "order": 31,
      "name": "Mutation runs become ledger nodes with verdicts, so a prediction cannot pass as a finding",
      "url": "https://github.com/turbophp/doccum/issues/150",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "size": "S",
      "release": "v1.0.0",
      "dependsOn": [
        "item/trash-view"
      ],
      "doneWhen": "A Mutation node type exists on the Ledger, sibling to pullRequests and decisions, carrying implements, pullRequest, run, headSha, mutant (what was broken), check (the named assertion that must trip) and verdict, one of negative (the check went red -- the good outcome), positive (the guard is not load-bearing) or void (the run died before reaching the assertion, so it is evidence of nothing). LedgerValidator enforces three rules: a Completed item based on spec:10 must be implements-referenced by at least one negative Mutation with a non-empty check; a positive or void Mutation blocks its item's Completion until a later one supersedes it; and check is required on a negative verdict, because a failure that names no assertion is not evidence. Decision gains an evidence term referencing Mutation nodes, so a decision claiming a guard is load-bearing either cites a run or visibly does not -- and the absence is the signal, no rule needed. PullRequest gains mainRunUrl and mainConclusion, and a Run may not reach outcome completed while a PR merged in it lacks mainConclusion, which makes LOOP step 3's claim about main's ref a recorded fact rather than a habit. The six historical mutation PRs (118, 122, 130, 132, 141, 145, 148, 149) are back-filled with verdicts read from their workflow runs; existing PR node names are left alone, because history is append-only. The 39 mechanical entries in mutations.json get no nodes: mutation-check.php re-proves them every CI run, so their evidence is the green tests run itself.",
      "actionStatus": "PotentialActionStatus",
      "startTime": null,
      "endTime": null,
      "result": []
    },
    {
      "@id": "item/home-dashboard",
