    {
      "@id": "decision/0032",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T01:08:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "Livewire does not scope its model-property restoration, and adding a guard can de-fang an older one",
      "rationale": "Two findings from item/file-policy-trashed-file-guards, both measured, and the second has no precedent in this ledger.\n\nFIRST: Livewire's restoration of a public Model property does NOT apply the SoftDeletingScope. It hands the trashed model back, so Browser::renameFile() reaches authorize('update', ...) and the policy is what refuses. The test was written to assert a SPECIFIC status because 403 and 404 mean opposite things here -- 403 that the policy refused, 404 that renameFile()'s own null check answered because the model was never hydrated -- and assertForbidden()-or-404 would have passed in both worlds and settled nothing. CI answered 403 green on the first run across five test jobs. Issue 116 was therefore REACHABLE rather than theoretical: a file trashed by another user between two of this user's requests really would have been renameable. It also retroactively vindicates replace()'s guard, which had rested on this same unread fact since item/files-versions-replace.\n\nWhat makes it worth a Decision rather than a code comment is that the reasoning went the other way, twice and independently. The implementing worker argued for 404 from the implicit-route-model-binding analogy, said so in its own report, and then flagged that it had asserted 403 anyway -- an honest contradiction it could have quietly smoothed over. The design consult argued for 404 from newQueryForRestoration(). Neither could read Livewire's source, because there is no vendor/ in this environment. Both were wrong. That is the third time in this loop a plausible chain of reasoning about framework internals has been overturned by a single CI run (decision/0028 and decision/0030 are the others), and the pattern is now firm enough to act on: a claim about framework behaviour that cannot be read here is not an argument to be won, it is a measurement to be taken, and the cheapest measurement is a test that asserts the discriminating observable.\n\nThe item's own shape is the generalisable part. The test was SPLIT: a guarantee half asserting the security property (a file trashed on its own is not renameable through the browser), which is true under both mechanisms and stays green whichever CI reported; and a discriminant half asserting the one thing that differs. Conflating them is what forces a coin flip, and a coin flip on an assertion is how an unverified claim gets written down as a fact. Separating them means the guarantee never depends on the unknown, and the unknown gets settled without risking the guarantee.\n\nSECOND, and this one is new: ADDING A CORRECT GUARD CAN SILENTLY DE-FANG AN OLDER GUARD'S PROOF. The guards job failed its first round not on any of the three new mutations -- all three passed -- but on three PRE-EXISTING ones: file-policy-{update,move,legal-hold}-refuses-trashed-directory all reported \"STILL PASSES with the guard deleted\". TrashDirectory cascades onto every descendant file, so a cascade-trashed file is itself trashed; the new trashed-FILE guard runs before liveDirectory() and refuses on its own; and deleting the directory guard stopped changing the outcome. Three tests that were genuinely load-bearing the day before stopped being so, with no change to their own code, and the suite stayed green the entire time.\n\nThis is over-determination arriving from a direction the loop had not considered. Every previous instance (run/0014's browser-select-directory-view, run/0015's, and replace()'s own) was a test that never isolated its guard to begin with. This one was correct when written and was invalidated by a later, unrelated-looking, entirely correct change. It follows that a mutation entry is not a permanent certificate: it is a claim about the code as it stood, and any new refusal placed EARLIER in the same method can void it. The practical rule is that adding a guard to a method means re-reading every existing mutation entry for that method, and the guards job is what makes that mechanical rather than a matter of remembering -- it is the only instrument in this project that can see the failure at all.\n\nThe fix is the isolation replace()'s equivalent test already carried: restore the file, leave the directory trashed, and assert $live->trashed() is false before the real assertion so the premise cannot rot silently. It is a reachable state rather than a contrivance, because restore(), delete() and purge() resolve the directory withTrashed() precisely so a cascade-trashed file can be restored one at a time.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/file-policy-trashed-file-guards",
        "item/files-versions-replace",
        "item/file-policy-null-directory"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0033",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T01:55:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A smoke check that leaves the browser mid-navigation blames the next check",
      "rationale": "item/files-three-pane's breadcrumb check took four CI rounds and only the fourth produced evidence. The three before it are the finding.\n\nROUNDS 1-2. The check failed IDENTICALLY on the correct build and the mutated one, deterministically, three runs, same locator. A mutation that reddens both directions distinguishes nothing, so nothing was recorded -- which is the rule working, since the tempting reading was \"mutated build fails, therefore the assertion is load-bearing\". Two diagnoses were wrong before measurement: a stale DirectoryAccess memoisation (disproved by reading the provider, where Directory::saved($flushAccess) is registered) and a flake (disproved by three identical failures; CLAUDE.md is explicit that flake is a diagnosis to be earned).\n\nThe cause was a wait that did not discriminate. After clicking into a subdirectory the check waited for the 'New folder' field to be visible -- and that field is on EVERY directory page, so the wait was satisfied by the page being left, before wire:navigate swapped the DOM. fill() then wrote into a field about to be destroyed and the subdirectory was never created. This is decision/0012's topbar lesson in different clothes: a signal already true before the action proves nothing about the action. The comment being replaced even claimed it waited on \"the NEXT page's own content\" -- the intent was right and the chosen signal did not discriminate, which is precisely why that lesson keeps needing to be relearned rather than remembered.\n\nROUND 3 IS THE PART WORTH CARRYING FORWARD. With the two navigations fixed the check passed, and then a DIFFERENT check failed: the upload guard, reporting \"issue #106 is reachable again\". That guard was present and correct at browser.blade.php:223-224 and nothing about it had changed. The breadcrumb check's FINAL wait was the same non-discriminating kind, so the function returned mid-navigation; the upload check then began on the outgoing page, its upload fired, and wire:navigate swapped in a fresh Upload button with no upload in flight, which reads enabled.\n\nSo a non-discriminating wait does not merely fail its own check. It hands a FALSE ACCUSATION to whatever runs next, naming a security guard that was never broken. A flake costs a round; this sends the next reader into the wrong file with a plausible story. It was caught only because the blade was read before the message was believed, which is the same discipline that caught decision/0032's over-determination and decision/0030's misattributed race.\n\nThe rule, stated so it need not be rediscovered: EVERY page transition in a smoke check waits on something true only at the destination, and a check may not return while a navigation it started is still in flight. Two corollaries. First, never wait on the thing under test -- the breadcrumb was both the subject and part of the navigation wait, which is why a breadcrumb defect surfaced as an opaque locator timeout instead of at the count assertion written to catch it; removing that dependency is what moved the mutated failure to a named \"got 1\". Second, when a wait of this shape is found, audit every transition in the function rather than the ones observed failing: two were fixed in one round and the third, untouched because it had not yet failed, produced round four.\n\nThe evidence that finally counted: correct build green with \"breadcrumb shows all 3 ancestors two levels into the tree\", mutated build red with \"expected 3 breadcrumb items ... got 1\". The count assertion is load-bearing, measured rather than predicted -- the implementing worker had named it as its best guess and explicitly declined to claim it proven, which was the right posture and is why the claim was checked instead of inherited.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface",
        "spec:13-infrastructure"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/files-three-pane",
        "item/container-smoke-ci",
        "item/upload-silent-discard"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0034",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T02:42:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A UI item that makes a latent policy gap reachable must close it, and a mutation must break the step the check can still reach past",
      "rationale": "Two findings from item/directory-access-ui, and the first is the one that mattered.\n\nTHE ESCALATION. DirectoryPolicy::manageAccess() required only AccessLevel::Manage, with no Spatie permission. move(), delete() and restore() all require directories.manage, and spec §5 groups them as ONE capability -- \"grant/revoke access, move or delete the directory itself\" -- so this was the single sibling skipping layer 1, against CLAUDE.md's binding two-layer rule. It was the worst one to leave open, because granting access is how every other restriction gets handed to someone else.\n\nIt was reachable by every user rather than in some corner: MEMBER_PERMISSIONS carries no directories.manage, and spec §5 also says every user holds manage on their own home directory through an ordinary grant. So any member could hand anyone any level on their own subtree while being refused the move and delete the same sentence groups with it.\n\nThe rule this establishes is about WHEN to fix rather than what: the gap was latent only because nothing called the ability from the UI, and this item is precisely what would have made it live. Shipping the UI and filing the hole would have meant knowingly shipping the escalation, so a UI item that makes a latent policy gap reachable closes it in the same change. Nothing was taken away by doing so -- with no UI, nobody could grant through it -- but it did settle a product question by implication, that sharing is an administrator's act in v1, and that is filed as issue 144 rather than left as an inference. If self-service sharing is wanted the answer is a permission of its own that members hold, never a layer removed.\n\nThe implementing worker found this and refused to resolve it inside the item, flagging it instead. That was the right call twice over: it was not the worker's decision to make, and the flag is what made the fix land with the UI rather than after it.\n\nTwo fixture traps came with it, both of which would have produced a green test proving nothing. A permission-alone fixture must NOT be an admin, because DirectoryAccess::resolve() short-circuits directories.view-all to AccessLevel::Manage on every directory, so an admin passes the level check by bypass. And the mutation anchor for the new clause had to sit INSIDE the return expression: the clause is textually identical to restore()'s and delete()'s and mutation-check.php matches by substring, so a bare anchor hit three sites, while anchoring above the return would leave a statement with no return and the mutation would \"fail\" as a TypeError -- testing that PHP has types rather than that the test catches a missing layer. Verified by applying the remove string and running php -l on the result.\n\nSECOND: MUTATE THE STEP THE CHECK CAN STILL REACH PAST. A smoke check with two stages -- grant then revoke -- has two candidate mutations and they are not equivalent. Breaking the FIRST leaves nothing for the second to act on, so the check dies on a locator before any assertion runs and the failure names nothing; that is exactly how item/files-three-pane's first two mutation rounds were wasted (decision/0033). Breaking the SECOND lets the first half pass and PRINT, so the failure arrives at a named assertion with the first half's success as context. The mutated run reads \"granting access through the panel wrote exactly one DirectoryGrant row -- OK\" followed by \"revoking access through the panel did not remove the DirectoryGrant row -- raw output: COUNT:1\", and the first line is what makes the second attributable to the one line that changed rather than merely co-occurring with it.\n\nRecorded with a correction that belongs on the record: decision/0033's rule -- never wait on the thing under test -- was broken here, one item after being written, by Promise.all-ing the Revoke click with a wait for the grant row to become detached, which is precisely what revoking does. The first mutation round therefore failed at \"locator.waitFor: Timeout 10000ms exceeded ... 25 x locator resolved to visible <li data-test=\\\"directory-grant-row\\\">\" instead of at the assertion. Knowing a rule is not the same as applying it; it took meeting the identical opaque-timeout shape a second time to look for the pattern rather than recognise it afterwards. Database evidence first and DOM second, as checkBulkTrashLeavesUnselectedFilesAlone()'s docblock already said.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control",
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/directory-access-ui",
        "item/files-three-pane",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0035",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T03:25:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A mutation that dies before its assertion is void, not negative -- and a fix that removes a proof must say so, then re-earn it",
      "rationale": "Three findings from item/trash-view, and the order they arrived in is the finding about the loop itself: the mutation meant to prove the item's own assertion instead exposed a defect in the harness, which exposed an unproven claim underneath it.\n\nA MUTATION THAT DIES BEFORE ITS ASSERTION IS VOID, NOT NEGATIVE. PR 148 neutered PurgeFile::handle() and its image job went red -- which looks, at a glance, exactly like the verdict it was opened to collect. It was not. It died in checkTrashViewRestoreAndPurge()'s SETUP, on the second of its two uploads, before either click. Red is not evidence; red AT THE NAMED ASSERTION is. Reading the conclusion rather than the log would have recorded a proof that did not exist, and it is worth saying how close that was: the run failed, on the mutation branch, in the check the mutation targets. decision/0033 already says a mutation that reddens both directions distinguishes nothing; this is its neighbour -- a mutation that never reaches its assertion distinguishes nothing either, and it is harder to notice, because the failure is in the right file.\n\nAN ATTRIBUTE CANNOT SAY WHICH UPLOAD FINISHED. The cause was clickOnceUploadSettles(), added by PR 129 to fix the race decision/0030 records. It timed the submit click off the submit button's disabled attribute: wait for the edge, wait for it to clear, click. checkTrashViewRestoreAndPurge() is the first check that uploads TWO files into one page, and a poll that latches onto the first enable it sees cannot tell \"this upload has finished\" from \"some upload has finished\" -- neither the tail of the previous upload nor a gap between _startUpload's response and the upload POST is distinguishable from the real edge. The probe caught it exactly: the submit commit went out 67ms BEFORE the bytes were acknowledged, so store() ran against an empty property, which is issue 106's discard reached through the harness rather than the product. setFileAndWaitForUpload() now waits on the upload's own request lifecycle, armed before setInputFiles() because a file input posts on change. The general form: wait on the lifecycle of the thing you are waiting for, never on a rendering of its state, because a state can be produced by more than one cause and a request cannot.\n\nA FIX THAT REMOVES A PROOF MUST SAY SO, AND THEN RE-EARN IT. That repair had a cost that was nearly missed, and was caught only while correcting the helper's docblock rather than while writing the fix: with the bytes acknowledged before the click, uploadAndProveStored() is no longer sensitive to the product guard at all -- an undisabled button stores fine there. Its docblock had claimed the opposite in as many words (\"if the guard is removed ... some upload in this run stores nothing and the run says so\"), which was true before the fix and false after it. That left issue 106's entire guard resting on ONE assertion, in checkUploadButtonIsDisabledWhileTheFileIsStillUploading() -- and decision/0030 had called that assertion load-bearing on reasoning, with no run behind it. Tolerable while a second check was also sensitive; not tolerable once it was the only one. PR 149 removed wire:loading.attr=\"disabled\" wire:target=\"upload\" from the Upload button and nothing else, and the check failed with its own message, after printing its control line. The claim is now on a run. It also answered the half the check's own error text cannot distinguish: Flux does forward wire:loading.attr to the real button, since removing the attribute is what turned it red.\n\nWHAT IS STILL NOT PROVEN, RECORDED RATHER THAN ROUNDED UP. RESTORE_LIVE:yes remains a prediction. It passed in both directions of PR 148 -- necessarily, since neutering purge is what let the restore half print at all and made the failure attributable. CLAUDE.md requires at least one shown-to-fail assertion per v1 UI item and PURGED_GONE:yes is that one; it is not stretched to cover its neighbour.\n\nTHE COST. Two mutation cycles for one item, one of them spent entirely on discovering that the first was void. That is the correct price. The alternative on offer was a green PR carrying a smoke check whose evidence nobody had, resting on a guard whose proof nobody had, with a docblock asserting both.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:10-user-facing-surface"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/trash-view",
        "item/upload-silent-discard",
        "item/container-smoke-ci"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0036",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T03:40:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "Sharing a directory is an administrator act in v1, and Manage on your own home therefore gates nothing beyond Edit",
      "rationale": "Issue 144 asks whether an ordinary member should be able to share their own home directory. It is a real question and the spec answers it two ways, so the answer has to be written down rather than left to whichever code was touched last.\n\nTHE TENSION, STATED. Spec section 5 defines the manage LEVEL as \"grant/revoke access, move or delete the directory itself\", and gives every user manage on their own home directory. The same section lists the permission set, and there is no directories.share in it. CLAUDE.md's two-layer rule is binding: a Spatie permission AND directory_access, both. RolesAndPermissionsSeeder gives the member role upload, delete, restore and directories.create -- not directories.manage. So for a non-admin member, the manage level on their own home now conveys nothing beyond edit. That is not a bug introduced by decision/0034's fix; it is what the spec's own closed permission list produces once the layer that was missing is restored.\n\nTHE DECISION: sharing is an administrator act in v1, deliberately, and the current behaviour is correct rather than incidental.\n\nThree reasons, in the order they carry weight.\n\nIt is what the product is. CLAUDE.md's first line says \"governed properties, permission-filtered search\". Governed. A system where any member can hand any level of their own subtree to anyone is a different trust model wearing the same UI, not a relaxed version of this one.\n\nIt is additive later and breaking now. Adding directories.share to the member role after v1 is a seeder line and a policy method. Removing it after people have shared things revokes a permission and strands the grants made under it. When one direction is reversible and the other is not, ship the reversible one.\n\nThe alternative would widen the spec without an audit. item/v1-audit walks the spec's permission list. Inventing a permission the spec does not name, somewhere in the thirties, is precisely the un-audited widening that item exists to catch -- and decision/0034 closed a hole created by exactly one such inconsistency between a level and a permission.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not a claim that the manage level is meaningless: an administrator holds directories.manage and reaches every directory through the directories.view-all bypass, so grant and revoke work in the product today, which is what item/directory-access-ui shipped and mutation-proved. It is only a member's manage on their own home that is inert.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE FIX, FOR WHEN IT COMES. item/directory-share-permission, post-v1: a permission of its own gating manageAccess() alone, never a layer removed from it, and never directories.manage granted to members wholesale -- that would also hand them move and delete on every directory they can reach. The distinction between \"may share\" and \"may restructure\" is the whole reason it needs its own permission rather than a reused one.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:5-access-control"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/directory-access-ui",
        "item/directory-share-permission"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    },
    {
      "@id": "decision/0037",
      "@type": "Decision",
      "dateCreated": "2026-09-18T03:55:00Z",
      "run": "run/0018",
      "name": "A guard whose null branch is dead in production proves nothing, and a remedy named before the evidence is the one reached for under pressure",
      "rationale": "item/download-missing-object was scoped as one change and was two, and CI proved that neither half fixes anything alone. The measurements, in the order they arrived:\n\nTHE HALF THAT WAS SPECIFIED. Open the object's stream BEFORE constructing the response, so a missing object can choose a status. Shipped on its own in the first round: every one of the six test jobs answered 500 where the tests expect 502.\n\nTHE HALF NOBODY HAD NOTICED. config/filesystems.php sets 'throw' => true on the documents disk, so Laravel's FilesystemAdapter::readStream() RETHROWS Flysystem's UnableToReadFile instead of returning null. The `if ($stream === null)` guard in DocumentStorage::readStream() has therefore been dead code in the shipped product for as long as that flag has been set. The typed ObjectMissingFromStorage the first round introduced could not be thrown by any real install, so converting only the null branch would have turned the suite green against a code path nobody takes -- a fix reporting success and changing nothing, which is exactly what CLAUDE.md means by a green suite being necessary and not sufficient. readStream() now converts both signals, because which one a caller gets depends on disk config rather than on anything the caller controls, and a provider seam owes its callers one answer.\n\nTHE MUTATION MEASURED SOMETHING THE ISSUE HAD ONLY GUESSED. PR 154 put readStream() back inside the streamDownload() callback, leaving the seam conversion in place and leaving FileVersionDownloadController untouched as a control. The control passed; the mutated route's test failed with HTTP 200, not 500. So with the stream opened inside the callback, the status line commits as SUCCESS before the missing object is ever discovered: on that server the failure is invisible to any client checking only the status code. Issue 134's reported 500 really was an artefact of FrankenPHP's header-flush timing, which the item asserted and could not show. A 500 would have been a weaker result than the 200 that actually came back.\n\nSo: open-early without convert-at-seam answers 500; convert-at-seam without open-early answers 200; both answer 502. Either half alone would have LOOKED like progress on the issue as written.\n\nTHE REMEDY I PRE-COMMITTED TO WAS THE WRONG ONE. The pull request body flagged the Storage::fake()/'throw' interaction as unverified -- correctly, since vendor/ is absent here and neither the worker nor I could read or execute the source -- and then said that if it went the other way the fix would be a one-line Storage::fake('documents', ['throw' => false]). That would have made the tests pass by configuring the fake to behave UNLIKE the real disk, hiding the production defect completely. Flagging the uncertainty was right and is worth continuing to do; naming a remedy in advance of the evidence was not, because the named remedy is what gets reached for under time pressure when the failure arrives. Flag the uncertainty, and say what would DISTINGUISH the cases, not what to do about one of them.\n\nWHAT IS NOT PROVEN. The Log::shouldHaveReceived('error') expectation rode along with the status assertion in the same failure and is not independently shown to fail. The status assertion is the one this item can claim.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "spec:6-storage-and-period-partitioning"
      ],
      "affects": [
        "item/download-missing-object",
        "item/download-reaches-the-browser"
      ],
      "supersedes": null
    }
  ],
